dict(case="Henoch–Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis)", qa=[
 ("Explain the pathogenesis (4-hit hypothesis).", ["Hit 1: increased galactose-deficient IgA1 (Gd-IgA1) in serum (genetic); hit 2: IgG/IgA autoantibodies against Gd-IgA1; hit 3: immune complexes form; hit 4: deposition in small vessels/ mesangium → complement (alternative/ lectin pathway) activation and neutrophil-mediated inflammation; triggers — infections (streptococcal, viral), vaccines, drugs"]),
 ("Adult IgA vasculitis — how different?", ["Less common but more severe renal involvement (nephritis 50–80%, CKD), more necrotic/ bullous skin lesions, higher association with malignancy (solid tumours — lung, prostate) and IBD, infections (HCV, HIV); requires malignancy screening if no obvious trigger"]),
 ("What is hypersensitivity (cutaneous small-vessel) vasculitis?", ["Single-organ cutaneous leukocytoclastic angiitis — palpable purpura on legs 7–10 days after drugs (antibiotics, NSAIDs, diuretics, allopurinol) or infections; DIF IgM/C3 rather than IgA; self-limiting after removing trigger; colchicine/ dapsone/ steroids if persistent"]),
 ("What are the ANCA-associated vasculitides and their skin signs?", ["GPA (PR3-ANCA): palpable purpura, nodules, ulcers, oral 'strawberry' gingivitis, saddle nose, sinusitis, lung nodules, glomerulonephritis", "MPA (MPO-ANCA): purpura, livedo, renal–pulmonary syndrome", "EGPA (MPO-ANCA ± ): asthma, eosinophilia, purpura, nodules on scalp/ extensors, neuropathy", "Treat with steroids + rituximab/ cyclophosphamide, mepolizumab (EGPA), avacopan"]),
 ("Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis — features.", ["Type I (monoclonal — myeloma/ Waldenström): hyperviscosity, retiform purpura, necrosis, acral ulcers on cold exposure", "Types II/III (mixed — HCV commonest): palpable purpura, arthralgia, weakness (Meltzer triad), neuropathy, glomerulonephritis; low C4, RF positive; treat HCV (DAAs), rituximab"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Lipodermatosclerosis", qa=[
 ("What is the role of ultrasound/ duplex in LDS?", ["Confirms superficial venous reflux (great/ small saphenous), deep venous insufficiency or post-thrombotic changes; guides endovenous ablation which improves symptoms and reduces ulceration"]),
 ("Why is biopsy discouraged and what if needed?", ["Biopsy wounds in sclerotic, poorly perfused skin heal poorly and may ulcerate; if diagnosis uncertain (e.g. morphoea, panniculitis, malignancy), take a narrow elliptical/ punch biopsy from the margin with compression afterwards"]),
 ("Describe the pain management in acute LDS.", ["Graduated compression (start gently as tolerated), NSAIDs, intralesional triamcinolone into painful indurated areas, pentoxifylline, anabolic steroids (stanozolol/ oxandrolone) in resistant cases, ultrasound therapy"]),
 ("What is the link between obesity and LDS?", ["Obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure and venous hypertension, reduces calf muscle pump efficiency, worsens lymphatic drainage — LDS is common in obese women even without varicose veins; weight reduction is part of management"]),
 ("What is elephantiasis nostras verrucosa and its link?", ["Chronic non-filarial lymphoedema (from venous disease, recurrent cellulitis, obesity, LDS) with cobblestone papillomatous verrucous hyperkeratosis of feet/legs; managed with compression, skin care, keratolytics, oral retinoids, treat infections"]),
 ]),
